        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column\">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>IT admins and CIOs should be aware that 2026 is effectively the final year of support for Intel-based Macs.\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.computerworld.com/article/4005201/wwdc-25-whats-new-for-apple-and-the-enterprise.html\">Apple told us so at WWDC</a>\u00a0last year when it said, \u201cmacOS 26 (Tahoe) will be the final release for Intel Macs.\u201d\u00a0</p>\n\n\n\n<p>That means macOS 27 won\u2019t support Intel chips.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>When Apple announced its plans, they might have seemed long enough away for some IT purchasers to ignore them. But with only a few months to go until Intel Macs lose support, it\u2019s now  time to think again and upgrade any old Intel-based Macs you or your business may still be using.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-bl",
